1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts

In *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* the hinge is developed through a set of architectural / performative sites that literally and figuratively mediate inside / outside, public / private and self / other.

The canonical hinge sites that Ellsworth returns to in the book are

* U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum, Washington, DC – the sequence of ramps, the underground Holocaust History Museum and the Hall of Remembrance as a spatial hinge between public commemoration and private mourning;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, Chicago – the house museum’s re-staging of domestic interior as public social-reform pedagogy and the porch/ street hinge between the settlement interior and the neighbourhood outside;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the programme that takes works of art out of the gallery interior and puts them on the street, and brings street life back in, as a material hinge;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s documentary theatre performances, most notably *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992* / *Fires in the Mirror*, used by Ellsworth as a performative hinge between audience space and witness space, between the stage interior and the social outside.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center and the work commonly referred to as Bravehearts: Men in Skirts are not used by Ellsworth as hinge illustrations in *Places of Learning*. The CSO Education Center is the distractor that is most clearly absent from the architectural case studies; the set of hinge cases that is repeatedly cited in the text is the Holocaust Museum, Hull-House, Children’s Museum Art Inside Out and Anna Deavere Smith’s performances.

2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts

Places of Learning is built around a set of museum / performance sites that are read as architectural / media pedagogies and as materializations of “pedagogy’s hinge” – the threshold device that puts inside and outside, public and private, self and other into relation and re-works personal and social boundaries.

The sites that are developed at length in the book are the museum/architectural hinges

* the U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum, Washington D.C., read as a choreographed threshold of entry, descent and witnessing;
* the Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, Chicago, as a domestic/social-settlement hinge between neighborhood, history and present use;
* the Manhattan / Children’s Museum of Manhattan’s Art Inside Out programme, as an inside-outwards move of art work from gallery to street;

and the performance pedagogy that is treated as a mobile hinge

* Anna Deavere Smith’s documentary theatre performances, notably Fires in the Mirror / Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992, as a performative hinge between speaker, witness and audience.

Bravehearts: Men in Skirts is not one of the case studies developed in Places of Learning. The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is the one institutional learning site that is discussed in the literature on the hinge in the Chicago context alongside Hull-House, the Holocaust Museum, the Children’s Museum of Manhattan’s Art Inside Out and Smith’s performances, and is not presented as an Ellsworth case study in the book.

